ac:T15ca,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the best free blogging platform?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the job. For a business that wants Google traffic on its own website, Outrank Blogs: your own domain or subfolder is free and there are no ads. For a personal blog with no traffic limit, Blogger. For a newsletter, Substack or beehiiv. For reach inside an existing audience, Medium."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I start a blog for free?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pick the platform that fits your goal, sign up, choose a design and publish your first post. Blogger, WordPress.com, Medium, Substack and Outrank Blogs all take a few minutes and need no credit card. If the blog is for a business, connect your own domain from the start; on Blogger and Outrank Blogs that is free."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which free blog site is best for beginners?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"WordPress.com is the easiest way into WordPress, the most widely used blogging software, with a guided setup; its free plan shows ads. Medium is even simpler: write and publish, with nothing to design. Blogger is free at any size but looks dated. If the blog is for a business, Outrank Blogs sets up hosting, design and SEO for you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which free blogging site is best for making money?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Substack, if readers will pay: paid subscriptions cost nothing to set up and Substack keeps 10%. Kit lets you sell a paid newsletter and digital products on its free plan, at 3.5% + 30Â¢ per sale, and Blogger lets you run AdSense or affiliate links with no cut. Mediumâ€™s Partner Program needs a paid membership, and beehiivâ€™s paid subscriptions and ad network need a paid plan. For a business, the money comes from customers your posts bring in, which is why your own domain matters most."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which free blogging platforms let you use your own domain?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only a few, as of October 2026: Outrank Blogs, Blogger (as www or another subdomain), HubSpotâ€™s free CMS (up to 100 posts, with HubSpot branding), Kit (a newsletter site rather than a full blog) and GitHub Pages (for developers). WordPress.com, Medium, Wix, beehiiv, Hashnode and Bear Blog need a paid plan or membership, Substack charges a one-time $50 fee, and Tumblr sells a paid domain connection."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there truly free blog hosting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Blogger, Outrank Blogs, WordPress.com, Substack, Medium and others host your blog for free. The catches are the address (often the platformâ€™s domain), ads on some free plans, and limits: Outrank Blogs is free up to 10,000 visits a month, WordPress.com gives you 1 GB of storage and Wix 500 MB. Free web hosts such as InfinityFree can run WordPress you install yourself, but they restrict commercial use and leave updates and security to you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I move my blog to another platform later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually. Outrank Blogs, Blogger, WordPress.com, Substack, Medium, beehiiv, Bear Blog and Tumblr all export your posts, but Wix cannot export blog posts and Kit exports only your subscribers. The bigger issue is the address: if the blog lives on the platformâ€™s domain, your links and Google rankings stay behind when you leave. On your own domain, you can switch platforms and keep both."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a free blog live at yourbrand.com/blog?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With Outrank Blogs, yes: a subfolder is supported on the free plan, with setup guides for Next.js, Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare and nginx. Self-hosted WordPress and Ghost can also run in a subfolder, but you pay for hosting. Ghost(Pro) offers it as a paid add-on on its Business plan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will a free blog rank on Google?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It can. On a platform address, though, your posts build the platformâ€™s authority rather than yours. If the blog exists to bring customers to your business, publish on your own domain or a subfolder of your website."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which blogging platform is best for SEO?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One that runs on your own domain and lets you set titles, descriptions and URLs, with a sitemap. Outrank Blogs does this on its free plan, including yourbrand.com/blog. Self-hosted WordPress gives the most control if you are willing to manage plugins and hosting. On Medium or a substack.com address, your posts build the platformâ€™s authority rather than yours."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is blogging still worth it now that people ask AI?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and a blog on your own site helps you show up in AI answers. In our study of 96 questions, 74% of the sources ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini and Claude cited were blog content, and 99% of those blog posts were on the companyâ€™s own site, not on Medium, Substack or a similar platform."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which free blogging platforms have no ads?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Outrank Blogs, Blogger (unless you turn on AdSense), Substack, Medium, Bear Blog and GitHub Pages show no platform ads, and Tumblr shows none on your blogâ€™s own site, only inside its dashboard and apps. WordPress.com and Wix show their own ads on free sites."}}]}42:["$","section",null,{"className":"section ","id":"faq","children":[[["$","script","0",{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$ac"}}]],"$Lad"]}]
